import{_ as e,o as s,c as n,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const o="/llamatron/assets/instruments.DIsQCBt6.svg",i="/llamatron/assets/instrument_editor.Behx3qZh.svg",y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Instruments","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"INSTRUMENTS.md","filePath":"INSTRUMENTS.md"}'),r={name:"INSTRUMENTS.md"};function d(h,t,c,l,u,m){return s(),n("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[a('<h1 id="instruments" tabindex="-1">Instruments <a class="header-anchor" href="#instruments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Instruments&quot;">​</a></h1><p>An instrument is where a track&#39;s notes go when the song plays in real time: a MIDI device and channel, FluidSynth, or a sound llamatron makes itself, a built-in instrument. This page covers the Instruments page and the instrument editor.</p><p><img src="'+o+'" alt="The Instruments page"></p><h2 id="what-an-instrument-is" tabindex="-1">What an Instrument Is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-an-instrument-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What an Instrument Is&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An instrument names a device and a channel. The device is one of this computer&#39;s MIDI output ports, with a channel on it from 1 to 16, or FluidSynth, the free software synthesizer the tracker starts by itself. Each track chooses an instrument in its track editor, and a track with none is left out of real-time playback. Two instruments may share a device and a channel, so one synthesizer can play every track, or each track can have a voice of its own.</p><p>An instrument can also choose a General MIDI sound, which FluidSynth plays and the MIDI (General MIDI) export gives the instrument&#39;s tracks. An instrument with a sound but no device is there for that export alone, and its tracks are silent in real time. When no track plays through an instrument with a device, playback doesn&#39;t start, and a dialog says why. No other export reads an instrument, since a Live set has instruments of its own.</p><h2 id="fluidsynth-instruments" tabindex="-1">FluidSynth Instruments <a class="header-anchor" href="#fluidsynth-instruments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FluidSynth Instruments&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An instrument whose Device is <code>*FluidSynth</code> plays through FluidSynth, as <a href="./FLUIDSYNTH">FluidSynth</a> describes. Its Channel shows <code>(auto)</code>, because each track through FluidSynth gets a channel of its own, as the MIDI (General MIDI) export assigns them: channel 10 for a drum track, where FluidSynth plays drums, and channels 1 to 16 in turn for the rest. Each clip starts by choosing the track&#39;s General MIDI sound.</p><p>A FluidSynth instrument&#39;s Engine tab also has a Pan, under its GM sound: where the instrument stands between the speakers, from 0%, hard left, to 100%, hard right, 50%, the middle, unless set. A band&#39;s guitar a little to the left and its keyboard a little to the right sound like players standing apart on a stage. Once set, each clip sends it as CC 10 after the General MIDI sound, and the MIDI (General MIDI) and WAV (FluidSynth) exports place the track there too.</p><p>Every example plays this way as it comes: every track plays through a FluidSynth instrument, with a drum kit for the drum tracks, so opening an example and pressing <code>Space</code> on Arrange&#39;s corner plays it. A new set has no instruments until you add them.</p><h2 id="built-in-instruments" tabindex="-1">Built-in Instruments <a class="header-anchor" href="#built-in-instruments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Built-in Instruments&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A built-in instrument plays a sound llamatron makes itself, with no device: its Engine is LlamaSynth. The sound is made on threads of its own, beside the one that sends MIDI, and MIDI and FluidSynth are held back by the few milliseconds the sound takes to reach the speakers, so that every instrument lands together.</p><p><a href="./SYNTHS">Synths</a> describes the built-in synths: their engines, each of their parts, the Envelopes, Filter, Modulators, Mod Matrix and FX tabs, and how the sound is made.</p><h2 id="missing-devices" tabindex="-1">Missing Devices <a class="header-anchor" href="#missing-devices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Missing Devices&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a set opens whose tracks play through an instrument that can&#39;t sound on this computer, a dialog says which: FluidSynth when it isn&#39;t installed, and each instrument whose MIDI port isn&#39;t connected, such as a set saved on another computer with <code>IAC Driver Bus 1</code>. The set keeps every device, so the set plays again wherever the device is, and the Instruments page can choose another.</p><h2 id="instruments-page" tabindex="-1">Instruments Page <a class="header-anchor" href="#instruments-page" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Instruments Page&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Press <code>I</code> on the main screen to open the Instruments page. The list shows each instrument&#39;s device and channel, and how many tracks play through it. A song plays in real time as soon as any of its tracks plays through an instrument that can sound: a built-in synth, FluidSynth or a MIDI port.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>Adds an instrument with no device yet, named <code>instrument-1</code> and so on, and opens it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ENTER</code></td><td>Opens the instrument.</td></tr><tr><td><code>C</code></td><td>Copies the instrument to the end of the list.</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>Renames the instrument, updating every track that chose it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>M</code></td><td>Picks the instrument up: <code>↑↓</code> carry it, and <code>ENTER</code> drops it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>S</code></td><td>Sorts the instruments by name.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Bksp</code></td><td>Deletes the instrument, once no track uses it.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="instrument-editor" tabindex="-1">Instrument Editor <a class="header-anchor" href="#instrument-editor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Instrument Editor&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+i+`" alt="An instrument&#39;s settings, playing through
FluidSynth"></p><p>The editor shows the instrument&#39;s Name and how its steps&#39; velocities play, then its tabs. The first, Engine, chooses what plays the instrument and holds what that engine plays by; a LlamaSynth instrument has seven more after it, Oscillators, Envelopes, Modulators, Filter, Amp, FX and Mod Matrix, and every other engine has the Engine tab alone. A new instrument is off. <code>Tab</code> goes to the row of tabs and back, and Left and Right choose a tab.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Name</td><td><code>ENTER</code> renames the instrument.</td></tr><tr><td>Default velocity</td><td>The velocity a step with none typed plays at on the instrument&#39;s tracks, 1 to 127, 127 unless set. A track&#39;s own Default velocity wins. An instrument that sounds harsh at full velocity is set once here.</td></tr><tr><td>Accent</td><td>What a step with its Accent on adds to its velocity on the instrument&#39;s tracks, 0 to 127, <code>+0</code> unless set. Each track&#39;s own Accent is added to it. See <a href="./TRACKS">Tracks</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Velocity</td><td>How loud every clip of the instrument&#39;s tracks plays, as a percentage, from 0% to 200%, 100% unless set, stacking with each track&#39;s, scene&#39;s and clip&#39;s. An instrument that is too loud beside the others is set once here.</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>Off, MIDI, Sampled (FluidSynth) or LlamaSynth: MIDI plays through a device&#39;s port, Sampled (FluidSynth) through <a href="./FLUIDSYNTH">FluidSynth</a>&#39;s General MIDI samples, LlamaSynth through the built-in synths; Off plays nothing. Choosing another keeps what the others&#39; fields hold.</td></tr><tr><td>Percussion</td><td>A toggle, <code>○</code> off unless set: a percussion instrument plays on drum tracks, through MechaDrum or FluidSynth&#39;s drum kit. It changes only while no track plays the instrument, which the status line names otherwise, and asks first, starting on NO, since the built-in synth settings give way to the new role&#39;s first engine.</td></tr><tr><td>Device (MIDI)</td><td>The MIDI output port, chosen with <code>ENTER</code> from <code>(none)</code>, then the ports this computer has now, sorted. A port the set names that has disconnected is still offered, so that the set opens without the port and keeps it. <code>Bksp</code> sets none.</td></tr><tr><td>Channel</td><td>1 to 16, as the device numbers them. <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> step it, and <code>ENTER</code> types it. On FluidSynth it shows <code>(auto)</code>, since each track gets its own channel, or <code>10 (drums)</code> on a percussion instrument, and can&#39;t be changed.</td></tr><tr><td>GM sound (FluidSynth)</td><td>One of General MIDI&#39;s 128 sounds, or <code>(none)</code>. <code>ENTER</code> opens a picker of General MIDI&#39;s sixteen families with <code>(none)</code> above them, where <code>Space</code> or <code>ENTER</code> on a family opens its eight sounds, <code>/</code> searches every sound, and <code>L</code> (listen) plays the sound under the cursor as a scale through FluidSynth, or, where FluidSynth is not installed, through the song&#39;s Preview instrument in that device&#39;s own sound; <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> step through the sounds by number, and <code>Bksp</code> sets none. A drum track always plays channel 10&#39;s drum kit.</td></tr><tr><td>Pan (FluidSynth)</td><td>A FluidSynth instrument&#39;s place between the speakers, 0% hard left to 100% hard right, <code>50% (center)</code> unless set. <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> step it, <code>ENTER</code> types it, and <code>Bksp</code> brings back the middle.</td></tr><tr><td>Keyswitch lead (MIDI)</td><td>How many milliseconds before its note a keyswitch is sent, 0 to 500, <code>0 ms</code> unless set. <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> step it, and <code>ENTER</code> types it. See Keyswitches below.</td></tr><tr><td>Latency (MIDI)</td><td>How many milliseconds earlier the instrument&#39;s notes are sent, 0 to 500, <code>0 ms</code> unless set, for a device that is slow to sound.</td></tr><tr><td>Oscillators, Envelopes, Filter</td><td>Each part&#39;s Type and its settings, and the Voice. See Built-in Instruments above.</td></tr><tr><td>Modulators: Mod 1 to 4</td><td>Whether each is on, its Shape, Sync, Speed, Rate, Fade in and Restart. See Built-in Instruments above.</td></tr><tr><td>FX: FX 1 and 2</td><td>Each effect and its settings. See Built-in Instruments above.</td></tr><tr><td>Matrix: Slot 1 to 6</td><td>Each slot&#39;s Source, Via, what it Moves and its Amount. See Built-in Instruments above.</td></tr><tr><td>Preview pattern</td><td>A button that opens the instrument&#39;s own preview pattern in the pattern editor, for designing a sound before there is any music. Each instrument has its own, kept inside it in the set file and renamed, copied and deleted with it; no list of patterns shows it. It starts as sixteen sixteenth notes running up and down C major from middle C, on a hidden chromatic scale so that any note can be written without choosing a key, or, for a built-in drum, a C on each quarter in the set&#39;s drum octave.</td></tr><tr><td>Listen</td><td>A button: <code>ENTER</code> on it, or <code>L</code> from any field, plays the instrument&#39;s preview pattern again and again until <code>L</code> again, as does <code>L</code> in the preview pattern itself. Edits to the instrument and the pattern are heard as it plays. Leaving the page stops it. An instrument that plays nothing, its Kind Off, is refused.</td></tr><tr><td>Keyswitch Map</td><td>A button: <code>ENTER</code> on it, or <code>K</code> from any field, opens the instrument&#39;s Keyswitch Map. See Keyswitches below.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="keyswitches" tabindex="-1">Keyswitches <a class="header-anchor" href="#keyswitches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keyswitches&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A keyswitch is a note that a plugin reads as an order to change how it plays, such as a string library&#39;s sustain, staccato and pizzicato, rather than as a note to sound. An instrument holds a Keyswitch Map of ten slots, numbered 0 to 9, each with a name and the note that switches it. A pattern&#39;s steps name slots, on the Keyswitch row described in <a href="./PATTERNS">Patterns</a>, so the same pattern works with whatever notes the instrument&#39;s plugin uses.</p><h3 id="keyswitch-map" tabindex="-1">Keyswitch Map <a class="header-anchor" href="#keyswitch-map" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keyswitch Map&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up, Down</td><td>Move through the ten slots, and down from the last slot to the OK and CANCEL buttons.</td></tr><tr><td>Left, Right</td><td>Right moves from the slot&#39;s name to the slot&#39;s note, under the NOTE heading, and Left moves back; the triangle stands before the one the cursor is on. Up and Down keep that place, so one slot after another can be named.</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code> or <code>F2</code></td><td>Names the slot, from the name or the note. A blank name leaves the slot without one.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ENTER</code></td><td>On a slot&#39;s name, names the slot. On a slot&#39;s note, opens the note picker. On a button, presses it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Bksp</code></td><td>Empties the slot, its name and its note.</td></tr><tr><td><code>O</code></td><td>OK: the ten slots go into the instrument together, as one undo step.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ESC</code></td><td>CANCEL: nothing changes.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A note is written in the set&#39;s octave labels with its MIDI number, such as <code>C0 (24)</code>. A slot with no note shows <code>(none)</code> under NOTE. A slot may have a name and no note yet; a step that names such a slot plays nothing for it until the slot has a note.</p><h3 id="note-picker" tabindex="-1">Note Picker <a class="header-anchor" href="#note-picker" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Note Picker&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The note picker is drawn over the Keyswitch Map and shows one octave of keys, with the octave picked apart from the key. Left and Right move along the keys of the octave, Up and Down change the octave, <code>ENTER</code> takes the note and <code>ESC</code> closes the picker with nothing changed. The picker opens on the slot&#39;s own note. For a slot with no note, it opens a half step above the highest note in the map, or on the lowest C when the map is empty.</p><h3 id="how-keyswitches-play" tabindex="-1">How Keyswitches Play <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-keyswitches-play" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How Keyswitches Play&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A step&#39;s keyswitch notes start at the same moment as the step&#39;s note and are sent before it, and each lasts as long as the note is written, ties included. For a plugin that needs the keyswitch earlier, the instrument&#39;s Keyswitch lead sends every keyswitch that many milliseconds before its note. A keyswitch moves with its note when a groove, a Rush or a Drag moves the note. Nothing else touches a keyswitch: a track&#39;s Transpose, the scale, a strum and every other generator leave the note exactly as the map holds it, and its velocity is always 100.</p><p>Keyswitches go to an instrument that plays through a MIDI port, and into the MIDI file, MIDI stems, MIDI clips and Ableton Live set exports. An instrument that plays through FluidSynth sends none, and the General MIDI file and the WAV file leave them out, since a General MIDI player would sound each keyswitch as a low note.</p>`,32)])])}const w=e(r,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,w as default};
